<article class="section weekly-static-page">
  <nav class="post-breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/weekly-diet/">Weekly Guide</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Pregnancy nutrition weeks {{ period.weeks.start }}–{{ period.weeks.end }}</span>
  </nav>
  <header class="hero slim weekly-hero">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Pregnancy nutrition guide</p>
      <p class="post-meta">By <a href="/about/#editorial">{{ site.author }}</a>{% if period.id == "wk1-8" or period.id == "wk13-16" or period.id == "wk21-24" %} · <time datetime="2026-09-25">Updated September 25, 2026</time>{% endif %}</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title">Pregnancy nutrition for weeks {{ period.weeks.start }}–{{ period.weeks.end }}</h1>
      <p class="hero-lede">{{ period.summary }}</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="content-card">
    <div class="content-head">
      <p class="content-eyebrow">{{ period.title }}</p>
      <h2 class="content-title">What is developing</h2>
    </div>
    <ul class="dev-list">
      {% for item in period.development %}
        <li>{{ item }}</li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="content-card focus-card">
    <div class="content-head">
      <h2 class="content-title">Nutrient highlights</h2>
      <p class="content-summary">These highlights organize food comparisons. Daily nutrient needs continue throughout pregnancy; priority labels are not trimester-specific intake targets or medical advice.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="nutrient-cards">
      {% for nutrient in period.nutrients %}
        <article class="nutrient-card priority-{{ nutrient.priority }}">
          <div class="nutrient-top">
            <h3 class="nutrient-name">{{ nutrient.id }}</h3>
            <span class="nutrient-rda">{{ nutrient.priority | capitalize }} priority</span>
          </div>
          <p class="nutrient-why">{{ nutrient.why }}</p>
          {% if nutrient.details %}<p class="nutrient-details">{{ nutrient.details }}</p>{% endif %}
          {% if nutrient.guide %}<p class="nutrient-guide"><a href="{{ nutrient.guide.url }}">Read about {{ nutrient.guide.label }} in pregnancy</a></p>{% endif %}
        </article>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </section>

  {% if period.id == "wk1-8" %}
    <section class="content-card" aria-labelledby="early-food-example-title">
      <div class="content-head">
        <p class="content-eyebrow">Food and prenatal examples</p>
        <h2 class="content-title" id="early-food-example-title">What can I eat in the first eight weeks?</h2>
        <p class="content-summary">Weeks 1–2 are counted before conception. Food choices can support nutrition before and after pregnancy begins, while folic acid has a separate, time-sensitive role.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="dev-list">
        <li><strong>A fully cooked egg for choline.</strong> The <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Choline-HealthProfessional/">NIH choline food table</a> lists about 147 mg in one large hard-boiled egg. Compare PregNut's <a href="/food/01129-egg-hard-boiled/">hard-boiled egg record</a> by 100 g; that chart is not the amount in one egg. <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/food-safety/foods/pregnant-women.html">CDC advises eggs cooked until yolks and whites are firm</a> during pregnancy.</li>
        <li><strong>Boiled lentils with cooked spinach for food folate.</strong> PregNut's historical USDA-based records list <a href="/food/16070-lentils-mature-seeds-boiled/">181 mcg DFE per 100 g of boiled lentils</a> and <a href="/food/11458-spinach-boiled/">146 mcg DFE per 100 g of boiled spinach</a>. These are food-folate comparisons, not the folic acid amount on a supplement label or a typical serving. The <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Folate-HealthProfessional/">NIH folate fact sheet</a> explains the difference.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="content-summary"><a href="https://www.acog.org/womens-health/faqs/healthy-eating-during-pregnancy">ACOG recommends a daily prenatal with at least 400 mcg folic acid</a> starting before pregnancy when possible and during the first 12 weeks. Food folate does not replace that recommendation. Check the actual folic acid amount and serving size with our <a href="/blog/prenatal-vitamin-label-pregnancy/">prenatal label guide</a>; ask your clinician if a prenatal is difficult to take or your care plan differs.</p>
      <p class="content-summary">If nausea makes full meals difficult, start with our <a href="/blog/foods-for-morning-sickness-pregnancy/">small-meal and hydration ideas for morning sickness</a>. The food comparisons above are examples, not a reason to delay care when you cannot keep fluids down.</p>
    </section>
  {% elif period.id == "wk13-16" %}
    <section class="content-card" aria-labelledby="mid-pregnancy-food-example-title">
      <div class="content-head">
        <p class="content-eyebrow">Food examples</p>
        <h2 class="content-title" id="mid-pregnancy-food-example-title">What could I eat around week 15?</h2>
        <p class="content-summary">This chapter highlights choline, DHA, calcium, and vitamin D. The examples below are choices to adapt, not a week-specific meal plan or a change in daily nutrient needs.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="dev-list">
        <li><strong>A fully cooked egg with plain pasteurized yogurt.</strong> The <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Choline-HealthProfessional/">NIH choline table</a> lists 147 mg in one large hard-boiled egg; its <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Calcium-HealthProfessional/">calcium table</a> lists 415 mg in 8 ounces of plain low-fat yogurt. These serving amounts differ from the PregNut charts for <a href="/food/01129-egg-hard-boiled/">egg</a> and <a href="/food/01117-yogurt-plain-low-fat-12-grams-protein-per-8-ounce/">yogurt</a>, which each show 100 g. <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/food-safety/foods/pregnant-women.html">CDC recommends firm yolks and whites and pasteurized dairy</a> during pregnancy.</li>
        <li><strong>Cooked salmon with a side you enjoy.</strong> <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/consumers/advice-about-eating-fish">FDA lists salmon as a lower-mercury Best Choice</a> and advises 2–3 four-ounce servings per week from that list during pregnancy. This is weekly fish guidance, not a special target for week 15. Compare the <a href="/food/15086-salmon-sockeye-cooked-dry-heat/">cooked salmon record</a> per 100 g. <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/food-safety/foods/pregnant-women.html">CDC advises cooking fish to 145°F</a> or until opaque and separating easily with a fork.</li>
        <li><strong>A calcium option without dairy.</strong> The <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Calcium-HealthProfessional/">NIH calcium table</a> lists 299 mg in a cup of calcium-fortified soymilk. Fortification varies, so check your carton for calcium and vitamin D rather than assuming every plant drink matches that example. Our <a href="/blog/calcium-without-dairy-pregnancy/">dairy-free calcium guide</a> explains other choices.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="content-summary">These foods can contribute to a balanced diet but do not replace a prescribed prenatal supplement or individual care plan. For more detail, read the <a href="/blog/choline-foods-pregnancy/">choline foods</a>, <a href="/blog/low-mercury-fish-pregnancy/">lower-mercury fish</a>, and <a href="/blog/vitamin-d-foods-pregnancy/">vitamin D food and label</a> guides.</p>
    </section>
  {% elif period.id == "wk21-24" %}
    <section class="content-card" aria-labelledby="week-food-example-title">
      <div class="content-head">
        <p class="content-eyebrow">Food examples</p>
        <h2 class="content-title" id="week-food-example-title">What could I eat around week 23?</h2>
        <p class="content-summary">These combinations put this chapter's iron, protein, and vitamin C highlights into familiar meals. They are examples to adapt, not a week-specific meal plan or a change to your daily nutrient targets.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="dev-list">
        <li><strong>Boiled lentils with washed raw red pepper.</strong> The <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/Iron-HealthProfessional/">NIH iron table</a> lists about 3 mg iron in ½ cup boiled lentils; its <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminC-HealthProfessional/">vitamin C table</a> lists about 95 mg in ½ cup raw sweet red pepper. Vitamin C can help your body absorb iron from plant foods. Compare PregNut's <a href="/food/16070-lentils-mature-seeds-boiled/">boiled lentils</a> and <a href="/food/11821-peppers-sweet-red-raw/">raw red pepper</a> records per 100 g, then use the portion tool for the amount you serve.</li>
        <li><strong>Cooked salmon with cooked broccoli.</strong> <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/consumers/advice-about-eating-fish">FDA lists salmon as a lower-mercury “Best Choice”</a>; <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/food-safety/foods/pregnant-women.html">CDC advises cooking fish to 145°F</a> or until opaque and separating easily with a fork. The <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/VitaminC-HealthProfessional/">NIH vitamin C table</a> lists about 51 mg in ½ cup cooked broccoli. Compare the <a href="/food/15086-salmon-sockeye-cooked-dry-heat/">cooked sockeye salmon</a> and <a href="/food/11091-broccoli-boiled/">boiled broccoli</a> nutrient records, and use FDA's fish chart to plan weekly seafood servings.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="content-summary">These are food-composition comparisons, not a treatment for low iron or a substitute for prenatal care. If you avoid fish, the <a href="/blog/vegetarian-protein-pregnancy/">vegetarian protein guide</a> has other options; the <a href="/blog/iron-foods-pregnancy/">iron guide</a> explains food pairings and prenatal labels.</p>
    </section>
  {% endif %}

  {% if period.notes %}
    <section class="content-card">
      <div class="content-head"><h2 class="content-title">Practical notes</h2></div>
      <ul class="dev-list">
        {% for note in period.notes %}<li>{{ note.text }}</li>{% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </section>
  {% endif %}

